VALUES ('2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 'cpt-practice-2', 'CPT Coding Proficiency Practice Paper 2', 'cpt', ARRAY['Telehealth E/M', 'Regional Anesthesia', 'Wound Repair', 'Nuclear Medicine', 'Microbiology', 'Cytopathology', 'Dialysis Coding', 'Ophthalmology', 'Modifiers']::TEXT[], 100, 'advanced', true, 2, 150);

-- ── Section: Evaluation & Management (20 questions) — Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('8cd1702d-19d7-46e6-a60f-5523e7d9d9bf', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 1, 'For a telehealth E/M visit conducted via live, interactive audio-video technology, which of the following best describes how the visit is generally leveled?');
VALUES ('8cd1702d-19d7-46e6-a60f-5523e7d9d9bf', 'e3c4f610-6884-421d-966b-21ad34d3d675', 'Telehealth E/M visits generally use the same underlying E/M code set/leveling criteria as in-person visits (time or MDM), with telehealth-specific modifiers/place-of-service codes identifying the modality — not a wholly separate code set or an automatic lowest-level default.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ef5614cf-37cd-45b6-a388-df388b61cdb7', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 2, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of modifier 95 (or the GT modifier, depending on payer) in telehealth billing?');
VALUES ('ef5614cf-37cd-45b6-a388-df388b61cdb7', '3cf194aa-a222-424d-8e58-4a10a2c785cc', 'Modifier 95 (and, for some payers, GT) specifically flags that a service was furnished via real-time, interactive audio-video telemedicine — distinct from modifiers addressing service reduction, co-surgery, or assistant-surgeon roles.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dee7a7e9-2802-42f6-82a2-fd8ee76fdc91', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 3, 'Which of the following best describes an ''audio-only'' telehealth E/M service, as distinguished from a synchronous audio-video telehealth visit, from a coding standpoint?');
VALUES ('dee7a7e9-2802-42f6-82a2-fd8ee76fdc91', '5552e013-f31b-4058-a8f8-7b67f4bc3b46', 'Audio-only (telephone) E/M services are coded/covered somewhat differently than full audio-video telehealth in many payer policies — an evolving area where coders should recognize the modality distinction matters, rather than assuming full interchangeability.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e0e6188e-fc83-449a-a01d-ed947596b79f', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 4, 'Which of the following best describes ''remote physiologic monitoring'' (RPM) treatment management services within the CPT Medicine/E/M-adjacent code set, as distinguished from the RPM device-data-integrity concept discussed in a general HIM/technology context?');
VALUES ('e0e6188e-fc83-449a-a01d-ed947596b79f', '23c50e65-d9ef-4612-a995-189e7219e854', 'CPT includes a specific RPM code family (setup/education, device supply, and time-based management codes) reflecting a genuine billable ambulatory clinical service — distinct from (though related to) the general data-integrity considerations of using RPM data clinically.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6866d397-9b99-4df7-abb1-5f9180932e69', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 5, 'Which of the following best describes ''chronic care management'' (CCM) code time-counting requirements, as a non-face-to-face, time-based service?');
VALUES ('6866d397-9b99-4df7-abb1-5f9180932e69', '8c39277b-d9cd-4ac4-aabe-69fcd5e360e0', 'CCM codes are genuinely time-based, requiring documented qualifying care-coordination activities and clinical staff time tracked over a calendar month — not a flat fee with no activity/time documentation requirement, nor restricted to physician-only time in the base codes.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1450fd3b-9b62-41f8-a266-9ebfd7b6a6c7', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 6, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of ''interprofessional consultation'' codes (e.g., a treating physician consulting with a psychiatrist by telephone/internet regarding a shared patient, without the consultant seeing the patient directly)?');
VALUES ('1450fd3b-9b62-41f8-a266-9ebfd7b6a6c7', '2e396b64-7922-4c73-aaee-6a4f9d332765', 'Interprofessional (e-consult) codes specifically capture a consultant''s non-face-to-face assessment/opinion time provided to a treating provider — billed by the CONSULTANT for their own time, without directly examining the patient, a distinct structure from traditional consultation coding.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3ee95638-d1fd-4ab9-8ee5-460719457290', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 7, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of ''transitional care management'' (TCM) codes?');
VALUES ('3ee95638-d1fd-4ab9-8ee5-460719457290', 'c3cff1b4-fa51-4a3a-9d00-274c2763cd3a', 'TCM codes specifically capture the post-discharge transition-of-care period, requiring both a timely patient contact and a face-to-face visit within defined windows, plus care coordination — billed by the outpatient/community physician managing that transition, not the discharging facility.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('aaea008b-5882-41f3-824d-6ca74af03edc', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 8, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of ''principal care management'' (PCM) codes, as distinguished from chronic care management (CCM)?');
VALUES ('aaea008b-5882-41f3-824d-6ca74af03edc', '11103487-99ea-4321-8229-1eb585979465', 'PCM specifically addresses management of a SINGLE complex chronic condition (often by a specialist), distinguishing it from CCM''s multiple-chronic-condition threshold — a genuine structural distinction between these two care-management code families.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a27253b4-e5a2-4abd-9868-71f50f762845', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 9, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for a ''brief communication technology-based service'' (e.g., a ''virtual check-in'' via phone or video, not related to a recent or upcoming E/M visit)?');
VALUES ('a27253b4-e5a2-4abd-9868-71f50f762845', '32238a78-93b6-472b-bee0-60eceff82e93', 'The virtual check-in code family specifically addresses brief, standalone communication-technology-based services that don''t originate from and don''t lead to a related E/M visit within defined windows — a distinct, shorter, and structurally different service than a full telehealth E/M visit.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a2b127c7-8f41-4180-883e-ece8fe68bb50', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 10, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for online digital E/M services (e.g., a patient-initiated message via a secure patient portal that a physician responds to with substantive medical advice, cumulative over a 7-day period)?');
VALUES ('a2b127c7-8f41-4180-883e-ece8fe68bb50', '2becda1e-88ed-4878-8c7c-d058b55231e8', 'Online digital E/M codes specifically address asynchronous, cumulative-time digital communication over a defined period (e.g., 7 days) — a distinct code structure from live/synchronous telehealth E/M visits or in-person office visits.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1324d4ea-6b64-4392-85a7-5021804b63d2', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 11, 'Which of the following best describes documentation requirements generally relevant to billing time-based non-face-to-face codes (CCM, PCM, RPM, online digital E/M) accurately and compliantly?');
VALUES ('1324d4ea-6b64-4392-85a7-5021804b63d2', '3578d0a6-3267-41bb-be72-f2413c1e9f7c', 'This reinforces the recurring theme (already established for CCM/RPM specifically) that time-based, non-face-to-face code families require genuine, contemporaneous time/activity documentation — not a flat presumption or ''typical time'' shortcut, consistent across this whole category of codes.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c7c8b2b8-091b-45bd-9fb9-9ad5e53a5e77', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 12, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient has both a chronic care management (CCM) service AND a principal care management (PCM) service billed for overlapping time periods by different providers (e.g., primary care billing CCM, a cardiologist billing PCM for a specific complex cardiac condition)?');
VALUES ('c7c8b2b8-091b-45bd-9fb9-9ad5e53a5e77', 'b1d83c33-71d0-4ffa-87d7-a2c168a037e4', 'CCM (multiple chronic conditions, typically primary care) and PCM (single complex condition, often a focused specialist) can potentially coexist for the same patient when each reflects genuinely distinct, non-duplicated scope of care management — not an automatic either/or restriction, but also not permitting double-counting the same time/activities toward both.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('af87f198-0efb-434f-8ac8-3b49e698f681', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 13, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of ''prolonged clinical staff services'' as a Medicine/E/M-adjacent code, as distinguished from prolonged PHYSICIAN E/M service time discussed in Paper 1?');
VALUES ('af87f198-0efb-434f-8ac8-3b49e698f681', '4b7018e6-c080-44f3-bff2-3d6186ff355e', 'This distinguishes prolonged CLINICAL STAFF time-based codes from the prolonged PHYSICIAN E/M time codes already covered in Paper 1 — genuinely different code families reflecting whose time is being captured and under what supervision structure.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7257f47b-5f4b-4f84-9a5a-dea5468105e9', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 14, 'Which of the following best describes the relevance of ''incident-to'' billing rules to services provided by clinical staff or auxiliary personnel under physician supervision (e.g., certain CCM or injection administration services)?');
VALUES ('7257f47b-5f4b-4f84-9a5a-dea5468105e9', '69591554-79c6-457d-8b93-e679ba70aa7c', 'Incident-to billing rules (governing appropriate supervision and billing-under-the-physician''s-NPI for auxiliary-staff-performed services) are relevant context for several of the staff-performed/non-face-to-face services already discussed — a foundational Medicare billing concept intersecting with CPT code use, worth recognizing even though this varies by payer.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dd6ed6c9-be70-48ac-9ce1-289164e478c7', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 15, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient''s telehealth visit is interrupted by technical connectivity failure partway through, and the visit is completed by telephone (audio-only) for the remainder?');
VALUES ('dd6ed6c9-be70-48ac-9ce1-289164e478c7', '36ba6d04-f938-4217-989a-c270aba88df1', 'Consistent with the general ''code what actually happened'' principle applied throughout this content, a modality change mid-encounter should be accurately reflected in documentation and coding/modifier selection — not automatically voided, nor treated as if the interruption never occurred.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('e1240219-a9e0-4df4-8486-5cf7b16b5418', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 16, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable practice for determining whether a given payer covers a specific telehealth-delivered service, given that telehealth coverage policy has evolved significantly and varies by payer?');
VALUES ('e1240219-a9e0-4df4-8486-5cf7b16b5418', '448353d8-e071-4435-b453-7017e974da22', 'Given genuinely evolving and payer-varying telehealth coverage policy, payer-specific verification (rather than an assumed universal rule) is the accurate, practical approach — consistent with the general theme of not assuming static, universal rules where genuine payer/policy variation exists.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3d65c403-0211-4f6f-9aaa-f4773551acd1', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 17, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of ''general behavioral health integration'' (BHI) or ''psychiatric collaborative care model'' (CoCM) codes within CPT?');
VALUES ('3d65c403-0211-4f6f-9aaa-f4773551acd1', '6ba73c64-7578-40d4-a6cc-a28551fbfdc8', 'BHI/CoCM codes reflect a specific, structured collaborative-care model (primary care + behavioral health care manager + psychiatric consultant) billed based on defined time/activity thresholds — a distinct code family from standard psychotherapy or general E/M visits, reflecting integrated behavioral health delivery in primary care settings.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b16d3ee1-3445-4a8f-915e-0352b3dc3b01', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 18, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support billing psychiatric collaborative care model (CoCM) codes accurately?');
VALUES ('b16d3ee1-3445-4a8f-915e-0352b3dc3b01', '29add5f3-d7eb-474a-bd9c-86087914880e', 'This reapplies the recurring time/activity-documentation principle (already established for CCM/PCM/RPM) to CoCM specifically — genuine, specific documentation of the care manager''s and consultant''s qualifying activities/time supports compliant billing, not a flat-fee assumption.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7a4694f6-c130-44b6-b011-20fadbb1773e', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 19, 'Which of the following best describes the general concept of ''advance care planning'' (ACP) codes within CPT?');
VALUES ('7a4694f6-c130-44b6-b011-20fadbb1773e', '1aa2477c-6f49-408b-adb8-1c2addcb9fc3', 'ACP codes specifically capture time spent on advance-care-planning discussions (a defined, billable service with its own time thresholds), potentially reportable alongside a same-day E/M service when both are genuinely, separately performed — not restricted to only terminally ill patients, and not automatically bundled into every E/M visit.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('81d9eac4-8619-4dc9-8682-d09273688712', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 20, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance consideration for billing advance care planning (ACP) codes on the same date as a same-day E/M visit?');
VALUES ('81d9eac4-8619-4dc9-8682-d09273688712', '50e9d382-0fa0-4b9c-9001-e598e4eec964', 'This reinforces the same-day-multiple-distinct-service documentation principle applied to ACP specifically — a brief mention of advance directives during routine E/M work doesn''t automatically meet the ACP code''s own substantive time/content requirements, requiring genuine separate identifiability to support compliant separate billing.', 'Evaluation & Management — authored from AMA CPT E/M guidelines including telehealth-adapted E/M concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Anesthesia (10 questions) — Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('d377b428-3d9e-4f9a-8a85-5dc288b8d490', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 21, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for labor epidural anesthesia during vaginal delivery?');
VALUES ('d377b428-3d9e-4f9a-8a85-5dc288b8d490', 'df774822-1584-4904-a47f-f09c5fdda68c', 'Obstetric (labor) anesthesia has its own specific code structure and, for many payers, a different payment methodology than the standard base-unit-plus-time-unit formula used for general surgical anesthesia — a genuine structural distinction coders should recognize.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f71e5e1c-b426-4ff4-967d-56f94d2c68cb', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 22, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for anesthesia provided for a cesarean delivery following a labor epidural that is extended/converted for the surgical delivery?');
VALUES ('f71e5e1c-b426-4ff4-967d-56f94d2c68cb', '67bcc842-7c98-4b15-a467-66da7fe56593', 'CPT''s obstetric anesthesia codes distinguish a converted-from-labor cesarean delivery anesthesia service from a planned, primary cesarean anesthesia service — reflecting the different clinical scenario and typical service intensity of each.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1260ee02-88a6-46bf-9d94-a45161d1e093', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 23, 'Which of the following best describes a ''nerve block'' performed for postoperative pain management (e.g., a femoral nerve block for knee surgery), as distinguished from the anesthesia provided for the surgery itself?');
VALUES ('1260ee02-88a6-46bf-9d94-a45161d1e093', 'dd94265d-4ba9-4ace-822b-51d47aa90b39', 'A distinct, separately medically necessary postoperative pain block (as opposed to the primary surgical anesthetic) may be separately reportable — a nuanced area requiring correct modifier use and clear documentation of the block''s separate purpose to support compliant separate reporting.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3c67ba34-40ca-4497-8632-dbb45bf32ddb', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 24, 'Which of the following best describes CPT''s approach to coding chronic pain management injections (e.g., epidural steroid injections, facet joint injections) performed in an outpatient pain management setting, as distinguished from anesthesia codes used during surgery?');
VALUES ('3c67ba34-40ca-4497-8632-dbb45bf32ddb', '95a487a2-1b39-472e-985a-00ded1cf1c4a', 'Outpatient chronic pain management injection procedures (epidural steroid injections, facet blocks) are generally coded via Surgery-section (nervous system) procedure codes plus applicable imaging guidance codes — a structurally distinct code family from the Anesthesia section''s surgical-anesthesia-service codes.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f5f20f32-a2d3-4c6c-8a29-854bfacabc27', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 25, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when imaging guidance (e.g., fluoroscopy) is used to guide a chronic pain management injection?');
VALUES ('f5f20f32-a2d3-4c6c-8a29-854bfacabc27', '166fe10f-f899-4e8f-8116-d33cf725a0f4', 'This reapplies the general imaging-guidance bundling principle (already established for other image-guided procedures) to pain management injections specifically — whether guidance is separately reportable depends on whether the specific injection code already includes it in its own descriptor.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('635857da-2cdc-4681-b695-c38bfdb2ec46', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 26, 'Which of the following best describes the anesthesia coding treatment for a patient who requires anesthesia for a procedure performed at an ambulatory surgery center (ASC) rather than a hospital?');
VALUES ('635857da-2cdc-4681-b695-c38bfdb2ec46', '9ef1caff-b3f9-48dd-8d0e-debcedd2c851', 'The underlying CPT anesthesia coding structure (base/time units, physical status modifiers, qualifying circumstances) applies consistently regardless of facility setting — facility type can affect payment/billing rules but doesn''t change the fundamental anesthesia coding conventions themselves.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('352d374d-6880-4b05-bc73-c0945d545b53', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 27, 'Which of the following best describes documentation elements generally needed to support correct anesthesia time-unit calculation for a chronic pain management procedure performed under moderate sedation (rather than general anesthesia administered by an anesthesia provider)?');
VALUES ('352d374d-6880-4b05-bc73-c0945d545b53', 'd23d1de5-34e5-4f5d-bea1-8ecf7138578c', 'This reinforces the Paper 1 distinction between moderate sedation (its own code family, physician-performed, time-based) and anesthesia-section codes (typically for a separate anesthesia provider''s general anesthesia/MAC) — applied here to a chronic pain procedure context.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ea522556-c096-42de-8294-d6850fa826e4', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 28, 'Which of the following best describes a peripheral nerve block (e.g., a brachial plexus block) performed by an anesthesiologist as the PRIMARY anesthetic for an upper extremity surgery (rather than as a postoperative pain adjunct)?');
VALUES ('ea522556-c096-42de-8294-d6850fa826e4', 'ecb45a63-0ac9-4cbb-9740-dccdb3e34c69', 'This distinguishes a nerve block''s ROLE in the case (primary anesthetic vs. postoperative pain adjunct) as the key coding-determinative factor — when serving as the primary anesthetic, it''s coded via the Anesthesia section for that procedure, not as a separate pain-management injection code.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('975840bc-ffa9-415b-a043-c4d76d1188ff', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 29, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when general anesthesia is combined with a peripheral nerve block placed specifically for postoperative pain control for the same surgical procedure?');
VALUES ('975840bc-ffa9-415b-a043-c4d76d1188ff', '0d313255-d6a6-41a3-939b-334b371c5eb8', 'This reapplies the general anesthesia-plus-postoperative-block coding principle already established, now specifically to a general-anesthesia (rather than the earlier example''s implicit anesthesia-type) plus peripheral-nerve-block combination scenario.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c6ad8cb4-4645-4df2-887c-8ac52ca19857', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 30, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable documentation practice to support separately reporting a postoperative pain nerve block distinct from the primary surgical anesthesia service?');
VALUES ('c6ad8cb4-4645-4df2-887c-8ac52ca19857', '913890e4-f43f-4c41-ba68-ed20bbefcf8c', 'This reinforces the general documentation-support principle (already established for many distinctly-reportable-service scenarios throughout this content) applied specifically to postoperative pain block separate-reportability.', 'Anesthesia — authored from standard CPT anesthesia section conventions covering obstetric/regional anesthesia and pain management blocks; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Surgery (30 questions) — Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('1dd648f1-62e6-40fc-8535-b293bff25f9a', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 31, 'CPT classifies wound repair (laceration repair) into which three general categories based on complexity?');
VALUES ('1dd648f1-62e6-40fc-8535-b293bff25f9a', 'fb9aad1f-bfe1-4080-a39a-4ed39598170c', 'CPT''s wound repair classification (simple, intermediate, complex) reflects the actual technique/depth of repair performed — a foundational integumentary coding concept determining which specific code range applies.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ec61fd17-6502-454a-a438-f5701d313df5', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 32, 'Which of the following best describes a ''simple repair'' under CPT''s wound repair classification?');
VALUES ('ec61fd17-6502-454a-a438-f5701d313df5', '7fbd7bd1-6c0c-4752-93d0-090c47dc140f', 'Simple repair is the most superficial category — single-layer closure of epidermis/dermis/subcutaneous tissue without the layered-closure or extensive-reconstruction elements that define intermediate or complex repair.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7bf17aab-9197-4ffc-aeb0-bd0b0af0eaa3', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 33, 'Which of the following best describes an ''intermediate repair'' under CPT''s wound repair classification?');
VALUES ('7bf17aab-9197-4ffc-aeb0-bd0b0af0eaa3', '835c9a98-5923-420a-832c-4524caf8de07', 'Intermediate repair specifically requires LAYERED closure involving deeper tissue (subcutaneous/superficial fascia) beyond simple skin closure, OR extensive single-layer cleaning of a contaminated wound — the key distinguishing feature from simple repair (single layer, minimal contamination) and complex repair (more extensive reconstructive techniques).', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3c97b52d-5335-4d45-aace-4012303b29ec', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 34, 'Which of the following best describes a ''complex repair'' under CPT''s wound repair classification?');
VALUES ('3c97b52d-5335-4d45-aace-4012303b29ec', 'e305c5f6-f970-44d8-a737-5a10dd041e7e', 'Complex repair is defined by techniques BEYOND layered closure (scar revision, extensive debridement, undermining, retention sutures) — not simply the longest lacerations, since even a long laceration repaired with simple layered technique would be coded as intermediate, not complex, based on the actual technique documented.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ee2d708c-402a-4f7d-adf3-a93bed321832', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 35, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when multiple wounds of the SAME classification (e.g., all simple repairs) and same anatomic grouping are repaired during the same encounter?');
VALUES ('ee2d708c-402a-4f7d-adf3-a93bed321832', 'ec4efb2c-ad51-4ef7-99d3-90024f162352', 'CPT''s wound repair convention sums the lengths of same-classification, same-anatomic-grouping wounds into a single combined-length code — a frequently tested convention distinct from how, say, surgical excisions of multiple separate lesions are typically coded individually.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('bfb3252b-169a-4bb3-87a9-e2c9f6ed67e2', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 36, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when wounds of DIFFERENT classifications (e.g., one simple repair and one complex repair) are performed during the same encounter, even if on the same general anatomic area?');
VALUES ('bfb3252b-169a-4bb3-87a9-e2c9f6ed67e2', 'be599bef-5a92-4ed7-b45f-4f653688a420', 'The length-summing convention applies only within the SAME repair classification — wounds of different classifications (simple vs. complex) are coded and reported separately, each reflecting its own appropriate technique/length.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d059ddba-9d42-4b25-8219-163449a891bc', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 37, 'Which of the following best describes the coding relevance of excision margins when coding removal of a malignant skin lesion?');
VALUES ('d059ddba-9d42-4b25-8219-163449a891bc', '3d9e1422-56d7-402e-970e-d13e06a2a26c', 'Malignant (and benign) lesion excision CPT codes are selected based on the total excised diameter (lesion size plus margin), making documented margin width a direct code-selection factor — a commonly under-recognized but guideline-clear coding requirement.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('f963bd9e-308c-4fe0-b901-d30d1439b4d9', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 38, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a skin lesion excision is immediately followed, in the same encounter, by a more complex repair of the resulting surgical defect (e.g., requiring layered closure or a flap)?');
VALUES ('f963bd9e-308c-4fe0-b901-d30d1439b4d9', 'a818e788-a724-4946-b7e7-304becc3c9c8', 'This reapplies the general excision-plus-complex-closure bundling principle (already established in Paper 1) specifically to the margin/excision-diameter context — simple closure bundles into excision, but intermediate/complex repair or flap techniques are generally separately reportable.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('295a25f6-3f8f-4616-afc6-00222a5ac8a4', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 39, 'Which of the following best describes an ''adjacent tissue transfer/rearrangement'' procedure (e.g., a Z-plasty or local flap) as distinguished from a simple excision-and-closure?');
VALUES ('295a25f6-3f8f-4616-afc6-00222a5ac8a4', '0a820461-b3cf-467a-b80d-1242dfd1b54c', 'Adjacent tissue transfer/rearrangement codes use a distinct AREA-based (not length-based) calculation accounting for both the primary and secondary defects created by the flap technique — a structurally different coding approach from simple/intermediate/complex wound repair''s length-based convention.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0a7f9546-9a7a-4567-ae43-1f6c3b2dc7a7', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 40, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a wound requires extensive irrigation and removal of gross contamination (e.g., gravel, debris) as part of a single-layer closure, without any layered/deeper tissue closure?');
VALUES ('0a7f9546-9a7a-4567-ae43-1f6c3b2dc7a7', 'df3f5e8e-e20c-400d-ace5-67eb7b0f25f2', 'This reinforces the earlier point that intermediate repair''s definition includes an extensively-contaminated single-layer-closure scenario, not just true layered closure — a nuance distinguishing it from the false assumption that repair classification is purely about the number of tissue layers closed.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8cd7e491-22c1-4b5b-a4a2-9e7d53b5b5e9', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 41, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for excision of a benign skin lesion, as distinguished from a malignant lesion excision code family?');
VALUES ('8cd7e491-22c1-4b5b-a4a2-9e7d53b5b5e9', '035e9f84-7673-4b50-8e2b-ed1f8b2a368f', 'Benign and malignant lesion excision codes are structurally parallel (both diameter/margin-based) but represent DISTINCT code families reflecting the different pathology — coding a malignant excision using benign lesion codes (or vice versa) would misrepresent the actual pathology, a real coding-accuracy risk if the final pathology result isn''t correctly incorporated.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ba0583be-486f-4ead-8b84-8107842be07e', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 42, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a lesion is excised, sent to pathology, and the final pathology report reveals it is malignant, though the surgeon''s pre-excision documentation described it only as a ''suspicious lesion'' with no definitive pre-excision diagnosis?');
VALUES ('ba0583be-486f-4ead-8b84-8107842be07e', '3818f4c6-998c-4f11-82da-acc18fa33359', 'This reapplies the ''final, definitive pathology result governs'' principle (already established for GI polyps) to skin lesion excision code family selection — using the pathology-confirmed malignant/benign status, not simply the pre-excision clinical impression, for accurate code family selection.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('57e6c19e-c46e-48a0-ad88-ea4bf2ac3d18', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 43, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when MULTIPLE benign lesions are excised from different body sites during the same encounter?');
VALUES ('57e6c19e-c46e-48a0-ad88-ea4bf2ac3d18', 'a46a6f89-8004-43a9-9f0b-3a7dc5e3b096', 'This reinforces the earlier-established general principle that lesion excisions (like most surgical procedures) are coded individually per lesion/site — a contrast with the wound-repair-specific length-summing convention, which is the exception, not the general rule.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4214f2fa-f025-4e8b-ac30-e210a6fbec2e', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 44, 'Which of the following best describes a ''shave removal'' of a skin lesion, as distinguished from a full-thickness excision?');
VALUES ('4214f2fa-f025-4e8b-ac30-e210a6fbec2e', 'e3e79963-cfe0-4314-b79a-52bcbe1d595c', 'Shave removal is a structurally distinct, shallower technique with its own specific code family — not full-thickness excision, and not requiring suture closure (and thus not paired with a separate repair code the way excision-with-closure scenarios are).', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('0f9e300f-3bdc-4d5c-9901-cac1f849ad1c', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 45, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for cryosurgical destruction of multiple actinic keratoses (a common dermatology procedure using liquid nitrogen)?');
VALUES ('0f9e300f-3bdc-4d5c-9901-cac1f849ad1c', '643fe633-c160-43cd-bc6d-7eed9a8f493f', 'Destruction of premalignant lesions (actinic keratoses) is commonly coded based on the NUMBER of lesions treated — a units-based structure distinct from the diameter-based excision codes and the area-based tissue transfer codes already discussed, illustrating CPT''s varied code-structuring approaches across different integumentary procedures.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2a49a158-0789-4d54-9554-8d35c3ddb11a', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 46, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a skin graft procedure (e.g., a split-thickness autograft) used to close a large wound defect?');
VALUES ('2a49a158-0789-4d54-9554-8d35c3ddb11a', '51a50785-81ea-4342-9016-a7dc4c0e479d', 'Skin graft coding depends on graft type/source AND area — a distinct structural approach from both wound repair''s length-based convention and adjacent tissue transfer''s different area-based (primary-plus-secondary-defect) convention, requiring coders to recognize which of these several area/length-based conventions actually applies to the specific technique performed.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ec290488-8b50-406e-805f-d1cf81cb5902', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 47, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a skin graft is used to close a defect created by excision of a malignant lesion, all in the same operative session?');
VALUES ('ec290488-8b50-406e-805f-d1cf81cb5902', '4ba11ba1-4d3c-4f55-8931-7d655ade3b84', 'This extends the excision-plus-complex-closure separate-reportability principle (already established for flaps) to skin grafting — a genuinely distinct reconstructive technique beyond simple closure, generally separately reportable alongside the excision code.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('40d51b4e-18b6-4100-9c7f-a68e9b2d804a', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 48, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a wound VAC (vacuum-assisted closure) device application?');
VALUES ('40d51b4e-18b6-4100-9c7f-a68e9b2d804a', 'ffa60ec9-1de2-441c-9464-d8f23864ca8b', 'Wound VAC application has its own distinct code family (often distinguishing initial application from subsequent changes, and by wound size/complexity) — structurally separate from excisional debridement codes, since VAC application itself doesn''t involve excising tissue the way debridement does.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('81ef7c55-566f-495c-af83-a886084d8f48', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 49, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when excisional debridement AND wound VAC application are both performed during the same encounter for the same wound?');
VALUES ('81ef7c55-566f-495c-af83-a886084d8f48', '2586fb2f-d658-4968-bcc6-3980a88c24a6', 'This reapplies the general ''genuinely distinct components, separately reportable with correct documentation/modifiers'' principle to a debridement-plus-VAC-application scenario — not an automatic single-code-only restriction, but requiring genuine distinctness and documentation support.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6f1649a6-13b4-4d20-87ab-1a50f00206d4', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 50, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for removal of a subcutaneously implanted device (e.g., a subdermal contraceptive implant)?');
VALUES ('6f1649a6-13b4-4d20-87ab-1a50f00206d4', '687cbca4-906c-4f19-abbe-ddfc52797a9b', 'Device removal coding, like device insertion coding, depends on the specific anatomic location/type of device and technique — a subcutaneous implant removal is coded differently from removal of a deeper or different type of device, reinforcing that device-related coding throughout CPT (as in ICD-10-PCS) depends on specific documented details, not a single universal code.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1f547ce8-4035-4580-bb67-8a46c4c96776', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 51, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for incision and drainage (I&D) of a simple, uncomplicated cutaneous abscess, as distinguished from a complicated or multiple-abscess presentation?');
VALUES ('1f547ce8-4035-4580-bb67-8a46c4c96776', 'e469d52b-9f7e-4bf2-a71d-655fa12142a9', 'I&D codes scale with documented complexity (simple vs. complicated/multiple) — reinforcing the recurring CPT theme (already seen in wound repair, lesion excision, debridement) that procedure complexity/extent, as actually documented, is frequently a direct code-selection factor across many integumentary procedures.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('6c253bd0-2dff-456e-85ea-a35c92ce4e32', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 52, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a pilonidal cyst excision, as distinguished from a simple I&D of the same condition?');
VALUES ('6c253bd0-2dff-456e-85ea-a35c92ce4e32', '6f863fdf-6fbc-4839-a28a-b795817a4d21', 'Pilonidal cyst excision (removing the cyst/tract) and simple I&D (draining without removing the cyst/tract) are genuinely different levels of surgical intervention for a related condition — each has its own appropriate code reflecting what was actually performed, not an interchangeable single code regardless of technique.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('3dcf7e7b-5fbe-4f57-97ff-ddf6ff4f5808', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 53, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a breast biopsy performed via core needle technique with imaging guidance (e.g., stereotactic or ultrasound guidance)?');
VALUES ('3dcf7e7b-5fbe-4f57-97ff-ddf6ff4f5808', '3eebe209-fd78-45b5-a342-63675791b5be', 'This reapplies the general procedure-plus-imaging-guidance coding pattern (already established for other image-guided procedures) to breast core needle biopsy specifically — recognizing whether the specific code selected already bundles guidance or requires a separate guidance code, per that code''s own descriptor.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('278c4055-6699-4be8-b392-bcc972da649c', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 54, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a sentinel lymph node biopsy performed at the time of breast cancer surgery (e.g., during a lumpectomy)?');
VALUES ('278c4055-6699-4be8-b392-bcc972da649c', 'e4c930d2-e1cb-43f2-8ac7-03660138957f', 'Sentinel lymph node biopsy (a distinct nodal-staging procedure) is separately reportable from the primary tumor excision when both are performed — a genuinely separate procedural component, and distinctly coded from a more extensive full axillary lymph node dissection, which is itself yet another distinct procedure/code.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('06ca933b-592a-407a-9270-72954dda192e', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 55, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a mastectomy that includes axillary lymph node dissection performed during the same operative session (e.g., a modified radical mastectomy)?');
VALUES ('06ca933b-592a-407a-9270-72954dda192e', '889170aa-e43d-4945-bec8-b5bf842dce46', 'This reapplies the combined-code-when-accurate principle (already established for combined imaging studies) to breast surgery — CPT provides specific combined mastectomy-with-axillary-dissection codes distinct from mastectomy-alone codes, reflecting the actual scope of surgery performed.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5f727cc5-8325-46e4-b592-d3ed6349b8af', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 56, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for placement of a tissue expander in preparation for later breast reconstruction, as distinguished from placement of a permanent breast implant?');
VALUES ('5f727cc5-8325-46e4-b592-d3ed6349b8af', '344bdc27-9542-45dc-8f29-fb75d07bcf0f', 'Tissue expander placement (a temporary, staged reconstructive step) and permanent implant placement are distinct procedures with their own respective codes — reflecting the genuinely different clinical purpose and typically different timing (staged reconstruction) of each.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b7cdbf31-aab4-4b0b-882f-55262bed49e0', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 57, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a tissue expander is later exchanged for a permanent breast implant, in a separate, subsequent operative session?');
VALUES ('b7cdbf31-aab4-4b0b-882f-55262bed49e0', 'bd49e88f-5c83-47a7-b784-1720572afae8', 'The expander-to-implant exchange procedure has its own specific code reflecting this distinct staged-reconstruction step — different from both the original expander placement and from a routine, non-staged initial implant procedure, illustrating CPT''s recognition of the distinct phases of staged breast reconstruction.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('aa59c922-f10a-4b2b-97e9-d19545a43c14', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 58, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for excisional biopsy of a single palpable breast mass, as distinguished from a core needle biopsy of the same mass?');
VALUES ('aa59c922-f10a-4b2b-97e9-d19545a43c14', 'fbeb2dd7-fefb-41ca-aacd-7537ad3fa6dd', 'Excisional (open, surgical) and core needle (percutaneous, minimally invasive) breast biopsy techniques are genuinely different in invasiveness and are coded distinctly — reinforcing the recurring CPT principle that specific documented TECHNIQUE (not just ''a biopsy was performed'') determines correct code selection.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('437f6a2d-8310-4941-b1a7-8159cd4e689c', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 59, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar integumentary/breast procedure code-selection question, based on the recurring themes established across wound repair, lesion excision, debridement, grafting, and breast surgery coding in this content?');
VALUES ('437f6a2d-8310-4941-b1a7-8159cd4e689c', 'ae682e42-ab60-4b5f-8621-3b9ec7e08932', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis for the Surgery section content covered across both papers: accurate CPT surgical coding consistently depends on matching specific documented technique/extent/anatomic detail to the precise code descriptor — a theme recurring across wound repair, lesion excision, debridement, grafting, transplant, and breast surgery coding alike.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a242a901-81d2-4a5c-82e1-390ff9501eae', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 60, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a lumpectomy (partial mastectomy) performed with pre-operative wire/needle localization of a non-palpable lesion identified on imaging?');
VALUES ('a242a901-81d2-4a5c-82e1-390ff9501eae', '877b361a-55db-4355-8658-0f0795e52613', 'This reinforces the Surgery-section capstone principle already established — image-guided localization procedures and the subsequent excision may be separately identifiable components (or bundled, depending on the specific code''s own descriptor), requiring the coder to match documented specifics to the correct code structure rather than assuming a single universal treatment.', 'Surgery — authored from standard CPT Surgery section conventions covering integumentary wound repair classification and margins; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Radiology (10 questions) — Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('859e79a7-775c-4e84-89c8-a4adee3aa5fa', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 61, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of a nuclear medicine bone scan, and its CPT coding structure, as distinguished from a plain-film X-ray or CT scan?');
VALUES ('859e79a7-775c-4e84-89c8-a4adee3aa5fa', 'c4904bc0-8742-4588-a8e4-a025083e5980', 'Nuclear medicine studies (using radiopharmaceuticals and gamma camera imaging) are functionally and codably distinct from plain-film/CT anatomic imaging — reflecting a different underlying technology and clinical purpose (functional/physiologic imaging vs. purely anatomic imaging).', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('716abecc-edaf-4386-955d-2eec30ca9c04', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 62, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a radiopharmaceutical (the actual radioactive tracer substance) is administered for a nuclear medicine study?');
VALUES ('716abecc-edaf-4386-955d-2eec30ca9c04', 'ebdeb2b5-648a-4610-8537-8996e9a9639a', 'This extends the general ''product plus service, separately identifiable components'' pattern (already seen for vaccines and, differently, for contrast materials) to nuclear medicine — the radiopharmaceutical/tracer substance itself is often separately reportable via HCPCS Level II from the CPT imaging procedure code.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('15c5813c-e4b8-4e93-9f49-e92a71fccecf', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 63, 'Which of the following best describes the general CPT coding structure for screening versus diagnostic mammography?');
VALUES ('15c5813c-e4b8-4e93-9f49-e92a71fccecf', '58f1dec6-c311-4447-98d1-39cd09068716', 'Screening versus diagnostic mammography is a genuine, code-and-coverage-relevant distinction based on the clinical REASON for the study — not an interchangeable or age-only-determined coding decision, reinforcing the broader ''screening converted to diagnostic'' concepts already discussed for other modalities like colonoscopy.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a4a00087-edf8-47fe-a083-f16403e92fc1', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 64, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a screening mammogram identifies a finding requiring additional diagnostic views during the SAME visit?');
VALUES ('a4a00087-edf8-47fe-a083-f16403e92fc1', 'b555bd5f-8d48-438b-88ab-32b7b5f773af', 'This reapplies the general screening-to-diagnostic conversion coding principle to mammography specifically — coding should reflect what actually occurred during the encounter, consistent with how this concept was already established for colonoscopy and other modalities.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('d3fc0683-4a7e-4170-acfe-513b252ad065', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 65, 'Which of the following best describes digital breast tomosynthesis (3D mammography), as distinguished from standard 2D digital mammography, from a CPT coding standpoint?');
VALUES ('d3fc0683-4a7e-4170-acfe-513b252ad065', '30cf910d-72fd-4830-8b61-55786eea26d4', 'Tomosynthesis (3D mammography) has its own specific add-on code(s) reported alongside the base mammography code — reflecting a genuinely distinct imaging technique add-on, not a full replacement code or an identical-to-2D coding treatment.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9aaf9238-9d48-4b57-874d-4d129572028f', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 66, 'Which of the following best describes a PET/CT (positron emission tomography combined with computed tomography) study''s coding structure?');
VALUES ('9aaf9238-9d48-4b57-874d-4d129572028f', '61b8bf84-3108-4952-b552-8c0a1c1a4dd7', 'Combined PET/CT studies generally have specific codes reflecting the integrated nature of the combined study — recognizing when a combined code applies (versus needing to code components separately) mirrors the broader ''use the combined code when it accurately reflects what was performed'' principle already established elsewhere (e.g., combined abdomen/pelvis CT).', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('015935d3-74b9-4c54-94e8-7fc9471e581e', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 67, 'Which of the following best describes a bone density study (DEXA scan) CPT coding structure, as distinguished from a nuclear medicine bone scan?');
VALUES ('015935d3-74b9-4c54-94e8-7fc9471e581e', '72728df3-b307-436e-bc91-2face69b58f8', 'DEXA (bone density) and nuclear medicine bone scans are clinically and technologically distinct studies (density measurement via low-dose X-ray vs. metabolic/turnover imaging via radiopharmaceutical) — each with its own distinct code family, not interchangeable despite both involving the skeletal system.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b07af758-a284-41b1-b697-8ad419ec499c', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 68, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a thyroid uptake and scan study (measuring radioactive iodine uptake by the thyroid gland)?');
VALUES ('b07af758-a284-41b1-b697-8ad419ec499c', '621eb0fc-b54d-466a-ad04-19aa3c8ff05f', 'Thyroid uptake/scan (nuclear medicine, radiopharmaceutical-based) and thyroid ultrasound (sound-wave-based anatomic imaging) are fundamentally different modalities — each with its own distinct code, reinforcing that imaging modality (not just the body part imaged) determines correct code selection.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('4a7df138-dfc7-4dc4-b6d9-9e51d563d24a', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 69, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a screening mammogram is performed and, based on ACR (American College of Radiology) risk categories or family history, additional screening breast MRI is also performed on the same date for a high-risk patient?');
VALUES ('4a7df138-dfc7-4dc4-b6d9-9e51d563d24a', 'e0e39bc3-563c-4802-94f8-23c4942d719c', 'Distinct imaging modalities (mammography, MRI) performed for a legitimate combined high-risk screening protocol are each separately coded per their own respective codes — not bundled into a single code or restricted to only one modality per encounter when both are genuinely clinically indicated.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('62e686b5-2dc8-4ee6-83a6-3e6da50f321e', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 70, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Radiology-section code-selection question, based on the recurring themes established across CT/MRI combination codes, nuclear medicine, mammography, and DEXA coding in this content?');
VALUES ('62e686b5-2dc8-4ee6-83a6-3e6da50f321e', '72a687ab-c428-4b8b-b346-131e830d27cf', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis for the Radiology section content covered across both papers — accurate radiology coding consistently depends on matching the SPECIFIC modality, contrast use, view count, and combined/component structure actually performed to the precise code descriptor, not assuming interchangeability across modalities or defaulting to the requisition alone.', 'Radiology — authored from standard CPT Radiology section conventions covering nuclear medicine and mammography; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Pathology & Laboratory (10 questions) — Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('c0c89b1c-aaa2-44be-92f8-d71a4c0a2445', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 71, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for a bacterial culture with sensitivity testing (e.g., a urine culture identifying a specific organism and testing antibiotic susceptibility)?');
VALUES ('c0c89b1c-aaa2-44be-92f8-d71a4c0a2445', '2b2ac9ce-29e5-45d1-a5b7-ec0f02782e79', 'Microbiology coding distinguishes the culture/organism-identification component from the sensitivity/susceptibility-testing component — reflecting genuinely distinct laboratory processes, similar in spirit to how CPT elsewhere distinguishes genuinely distinct components of a broader clinical service.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('9fe4a770-f9ec-40dc-b81a-4e1b8185b526', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 72, 'Which of the following best describes the general CPT coding distinction between a ''presumptive'' and a ''definitive'' identification of an organism in microbiology testing?');
VALUES ('9fe4a770-f9ec-40dc-b81a-4e1b8185b526', '5fc49283-ec76-4dbb-b944-a77f0d0df5f1', 'CPT microbiology coding distinguishes presumptive from definitive organism identification, reflecting genuinely different levels of laboratory testing/specificity — coding to the actual level of testing performed and documented, not defaulting to one or the other regardless of what was actually done.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5f41dd00-560e-42eb-923a-c29f62451168', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 73, 'Which of the following best describes a Papanicolaou (Pap) smear''s CPT coding structure, particularly regarding screening versus interpretation components?');
VALUES ('5f41dd00-560e-42eb-923a-c29f62451168', '59451c20-6ee1-4ef4-9349-047db33d24c0', 'Pap smear coding reflects both the screening/technical processing and, where applicable, physician interpretation components — echoing the professional/technical component concept already established elsewhere in pathology and radiology coding.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c855dd9e-837d-47cf-b41d-c794403aaf06', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 74, 'Which of the following best describes the coding relevance of the specific Pap smear screening method used (e.g., conventional smear vs. liquid-based cytology, and manual vs. automated screening with manual rescreening)?');
VALUES ('c855dd9e-837d-47cf-b41d-c794403aaf06', 'aed38f7e-e3ef-49ba-976d-a58ea2e69ba7', 'CPT''s cytopathology codes distinguish by specific screening method/technology (conventional vs. liquid-based, manual vs. automated with rescreening) — a genuine code-selection-determining detail requiring documentation of the specific method actually used.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('13bc931b-bde0-48e1-b1e2-007ec575557d', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 75, 'Which of the following best describes fine needle aspiration (FNA) cytology coding, as distinguished from a surgical (tissue) biopsy coding via the Surgery section?');
VALUES ('13bc931b-bde0-48e1-b1e2-007ec575557d', '42435cd5-6b95-44b4-a156-52227af220da', 'FNA cytology (cellular sampling) is structurally distinct from surgical tissue biopsy — reflecting a different sampling technique and specimen type (cells vs. tissue architecture), each with its own appropriate code family rather than being interchangeable.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('aa4c3f67-4bb9-4810-8da9-9192955bdf8f', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 76, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a Pap smear cytology result is reported as ''atypical squamous cells of undetermined significance'' (ASC-US) with reflex HPV testing automatically performed per the lab''s protocol?');
VALUES ('aa4c3f67-4bb9-4810-8da9-9192955bdf8f', 'bb015851-d0d2-4599-b849-47f327bef330', 'This reapplies the general reflex-testing coding principle (already established) to a concrete cytology scenario — the Pap smear and the reflex HPV test are separately identifiable tests, each with its own code, when the reflex test actually occurs per protocol.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2ea4e023-46d3-4354-93e8-ef26e15311fa', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 77, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a blood culture that is reported as ''no growth'' after the standard incubation period, as distinguished from one yielding a positive organism identification?');
VALUES ('2ea4e023-46d3-4354-93e8-ef26e15311fa', 'be950505-17df-4fae-8a88-d581281e5dbf', 'The culture CODE reflects the testing/incubation process performed, not solely a positive result — a negative (''no growth'') result still reflects genuine laboratory work performed and is coded using the same culture code(s), just without any organism-identification/sensitivity add-on codes that would apply only if an organism were actually identified.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('5ece245e-b357-47f5-b623-15394f84cb13', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 78, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for a molecular pathology test identifying a specific gene mutation (e.g., EGFR mutation testing in lung cancer) used to guide targeted therapy selection?');
VALUES ('5ece245e-b357-47f5-b623-15394f84cb13', '07c4b174-cc81-44bc-ab23-a8113c890b98', 'This reinforces the earlier molecular pathology coding structure (per-gene Tier 1/Tier 2 codes) with a concrete oncology-relevant example (EGFR testing) — a distinct, specialized code family from routine chemistry or general surgical pathology examination codes.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('ab5e824f-36f1-469f-a48c-4e97cab8a746', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 79, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for an immunohistochemistry (IHC) stain performed on a tissue specimen (e.g., HER2 IHC testing on a breast cancer specimen)?');
VALUES ('ab5e824f-36f1-469f-a48c-4e97cab8a746', '53b8d3d9-36c5-4701-b1ed-8bf00875bcbe', 'IHC staining is a specifically, separately codeable additional technique beyond routine H&E staining — generally reported per distinct antibody/stain performed, reflecting genuinely additional laboratory work beyond the base surgical pathology examination.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('7eaf6cb3-5ec4-4737-9004-766d6822a1fd', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 80, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Pathology & Laboratory code-selection question, based on the recurring themes established across panels, culture/sensitivity, cytology, molecular pathology, and IHC coding in this content?');
VALUES ('7eaf6cb3-5ec4-4737-9004-766d6822a1fd', '988c62fd-ab52-4ce2-8ea7-7d0275de3995', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis for the Pathology & Laboratory section — accurate coding consistently depends on verifying the SPECIFIC components/tests actually performed against each code''s own defined scope (panel components, reflex triggers, specific stains/genes), not assuming broad interchangeability or relying solely on an order form without verification.', 'Pathology & Laboratory — authored from standard CPT Pathology & Laboratory section conventions covering microbiology and cytopathology; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Medicine (10 questions) — Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('095bbafe-25c3-4d92-9e07-9bad1b69188b', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 81, 'Which of the following best describes CPT''s approach to coding outpatient hemodialysis services provided to an ESRD patient on a monthly basis?');
VALUES ('095bbafe-25c3-4d92-9e07-9bad1b69188b', '4d5f7e5d-1c56-4946-be06-300b844e528a', 'Outpatient ESRD-related physician services often use monthly capitation-type codes (reflecting the physician''s overall monthly management of a dialysis patient) — a structurally distinct approach from session-by-session inpatient hemodialysis procedure coding (which, on the ICD-10-PCS side, was covered as a CCS-relevant concept).', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8cbd1027-5b42-41c1-8155-d1fdd1f4c0bf', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 82, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for routine ophthalmological examination services (e.g., a comprehensive eye exam), as distinguished from using a general office/outpatient E/M code for the same visit?');
VALUES ('8cbd1027-5b42-41c1-8155-d1fdd1f4c0bf', 'b5013709-9859-4bfe-81d2-5da55c388c0a', 'CPT provides a distinct Ophthalmology code family as an alternative (not an add-on) to general E/M codes for eye-focused visits — a specialty-specific coding convention with its own defined exam-element requirements distinguishing intermediate from comprehensive exam codes.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('c7da859e-23ab-4cea-aca0-2255dba88298', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 83, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for fundus photography or optical coherence tomography (OCT) performed during an ophthalmology visit?');
VALUES ('c7da859e-23ab-4cea-aca0-2255dba88298', 'cf3bdc97-a715-4652-ba4e-4c28069534be', 'Ophthalmic diagnostic tests like fundus photography and OCT have their own distinct CPT codes, separately reportable from the underlying eye examination — reflecting genuinely distinct diagnostic services beyond the exam itself, each with its own specific code.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('dee8eef5-c49b-4e1f-9790-22b2b0535839', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 84, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for intravitreal injection of a medication (e.g., an anti-VEGF agent for macular degeneration)?');
VALUES ('dee8eef5-c49b-4e1f-9790-22b2b0535839', '579ec80e-013c-41dd-b3f1-54c75b6b87e4', 'This reapplies the procedure-plus-drug-product separation pattern (already established for vaccines/immunotherapy) to intravitreal injections — the injection procedure and the specific drug product are separately identifiable and coded components.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('35f4dd24-3d48-48bf-b2f9-5af27c21157e', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 85, 'Which of the following best describes CPT coding for cardiac rehabilitation services, as a Medicine-section service distinct from a routine E/M visit?');
VALUES ('35f4dd24-3d48-48bf-b2f9-5af27c21157e', 'fac8f01a-4220-4d3a-aab5-913290ad92e6', 'Cardiac rehabilitation has its own distinct CPT code family reflecting the specific monitored exercise/education program structure — not simply a general PT timed code repurposed, nor inherently tied to inpatient status, and coded distinctly from a routine E/M follow-up visit.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('981d730a-c099-45ea-bb88-9ed8a07040c4', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 86, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient receives both an office/outpatient E/M service AND a cardiac rehabilitation session on the same date by the same provider?');
VALUES ('981d730a-c099-45ea-bb88-9ed8a07040c4', '824cfc15-e866-45a6-9c58-c69fade737ca', 'This reapplies the general same-day multiple-distinct-service coding principle (already established for E/M plus preventive visits) to E/M plus cardiac rehabilitation — separate reportability depends on genuine separate identifiability/medical necessity and correct modifier use, not an automatic single-service-only rule.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('96776d16-478f-4cc9-a10a-1717ca23066c', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 87, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding when a patient receives both a comprehensive ophthalmological exam AND a same-day OCT test for a documented, separately identifiable reason (e.g., routine exam plus monitoring of known glaucoma progression)?');
VALUES ('96776d16-478f-4cc9-a10a-1717ca23066c', '4b3a0c09-75f0-43a3-92b6-a3de80fa55ab', 'This reapplies the general same-day multiple-distinct-service coding principle (already established for E/M-plus-other-service combinations) to ophthalmology exam-plus-diagnostic-testing specifically.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1748397a-bad9-486e-b983-b0856e5650ab', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 88, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for cardiac rehabilitation phase distinctions (e.g., Phase II outpatient monitored rehabilitation versus Phase III maintenance programs)?');
VALUES ('1748397a-bad9-486e-b983-b0856e5650ab', '39730706-956f-416a-9bd5-b82ea808f7ce', 'Cardiac rehabilitation phase (II vs. III) reflects a genuine clinical/program distinction often affecting both code selection and payer coverage policy — not an interchangeable, single-phase-fits-all coding treatment.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('a66582f4-89e9-48bd-a4fa-40cbd2876db7', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 89, 'Which of the following best describes correct coding for pulmonary rehabilitation services, as a Medicine-section service structurally similar in concept to cardiac rehabilitation?');
VALUES ('a66582f4-89e9-48bd-a4fa-40cbd2876db7', '87993fb4-b001-4d52-a06b-2169580e1dd6', 'Pulmonary rehabilitation parallels cardiac rehabilitation in structure (monitored, session-based program) but is its own distinct code family for a different condition category (chronic respiratory disease) — not identical codes to cardiac rehab, reinforcing that condition-specific program type determines correct code family.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('b6435399-7224-4b68-880d-1c12508b961b', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 90, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar Medicine-section code-selection question, based on the recurring themes established across dialysis, ophthalmology, injections, rehabilitation, and care-management coding in this content?');
VALUES ('b6435399-7224-4b68-880d-1c12508b961b', 'c6bbd502-45f5-4641-ac63-71c650d9152e', 'This is a reasonable capstone synthesis for the Medicine section — accurate coding depends on recognizing which specific underlying billing-structure logic (capitation, per-session, time-based, product-plus-administration, phase-specific) applies to the documented service, since Medicine-section codes are genuinely structurally diverse rather than following one single pattern.', 'Medicine — authored from standard CPT Medicine section conventions covering dialysis and ophthalmology services; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

-- ── Section: Modifiers & NCCI (10 questions) — Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank. ──

VALUES ('5d6b92f1-30a4-44dc-80e3-26478c5fedc1', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 91, 'Which of the following best describes the general purpose of an Advance Beneficiary Notice of Noncoverage (ABN) and its related modifiers (GA, GX, GY, GZ) in Medicare billing?');
VALUES ('5d6b92f1-30a4-44dc-80e3-26478c5fedc1', '6cbf50e7-b983-4a8e-a658-7dd1d3252180', 'GA/GX/GY/GZ modifiers communicate ABN status and expected-coverage information (e.g., GA = ABN on file as required, GZ = no ABN obtained though one should have been) — an entirely different modifier category from clinical-distinctness modifiers like 59, addressing beneficiary-notice/coverage-expectation status instead.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1bc156ee-248b-4bcb-9266-0ac534b64b8d', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 92, 'Which of the following best describes modifier GA specifically?');
VALUES ('1bc156ee-248b-4bcb-9266-0ac534b64b8d', 'b4564034-181a-4753-9d76-244104b870a4', 'Modifier GA specifically flags that a required ABN was properly issued to the beneficiary before the service — communicating financial-liability/notice status, distinct from clinical/procedural modifiers.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1d2ace40-70b5-498c-946d-f36b44d71194', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 93, 'Which of the following best describes modifier GZ, and why its use carries particular compliance significance?');
VALUES ('1d2ace40-70b5-498c-946d-f36b44d71194', '1723220e-8c7a-4b45-b7ca-15a133e5e109', 'GZ specifically flags a service the provider expects will be denied as not medically necessary, WITHOUT a valid ABN having been obtained — a compliance-significant modifier since it acknowledges an expected denial without the proper advance beneficiary notice that would otherwise allow billing the beneficiary.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('91f57baf-6bba-49a3-b968-35567bbf54f8', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 94, 'Which of the following best describes the general relationship between telehealth-specific modifiers (95/GT) and NCCI edits?');
VALUES ('91f57baf-6bba-49a3-b968-35567bbf54f8', '8f551911-16f5-48d3-b626-88e9a8015fa5', 'Telehealth modality modifiers and NCCI-bypass modifiers address entirely different coding questions (how a service was delivered vs. whether a code combination reflects genuinely distinct services) — they can coexist on the same claim when both circumstances genuinely apply, and neither one substitutes for or automatically satisfies the other.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('2ef022ea-2cc9-4f00-ad45-1c5b08742b57', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 95, 'Which of the following best describes a compliance-relevant reason to avoid reflexively appending modifier 59 (or an X{EPSU} modifier) to bypass an NCCI edit whenever a claim is initially denied, without reviewing the underlying clinical documentation?');
VALUES ('2ef022ea-2cc9-4f00-ad45-1c5b08742b57', '999d30b0-9050-4544-a6e2-a2ce33c80fd1', 'This reinforces the earlier NCCI-bypass-modifier compliance principle — a denial itself doesn''t establish that a bypass is clinically justified; reflexively applying 59/X{EPSU} to any denied claim without documentation-based justification is exactly the misuse pattern CMS''s guidance and general coding ethics caution against.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('85ad44f2-61c0-4cf7-9e5e-7914e2d9ab5a', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 96, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable claim-scrubbing/compliance practice specifically for claims involving BOTH a telehealth modifier AND a modifier 25 (significant, separately identifiable E/M service) on the same claim?');
VALUES ('85ad44f2-61c0-4cf7-9e5e-7914e2d9ab5a', '322a21a7-910c-4f34-957d-03f827659b40', 'Each modifier addresses its own distinct compliance question (delivery modality vs. same-day-service distinctness) requiring independent documentation support — one modifier''s presence/justification doesn''t automatically validate the other, reinforcing careful, modifier-specific documentation review.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('1dbb6a2a-87b8-4253-9d82-37c630cbadeb', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 97, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance consideration for a facility''s use of interprofessional consultation (e-consult) codes, given their relatively newer/less familiar status compared to traditional E/M codes?');
VALUES ('1dbb6a2a-87b8-4253-9d82-37c630cbadeb', '50760f84-2387-440b-afe6-62a3df0c0740', 'Given interprofessional consultation codes'' specific structural requirements (no direct patient contact by the consultant, verbal/internet-based consultation, consultant''s own time) that differ from a standard telehealth E/M visit, careful documentation review specific to THESE codes'' requirements is a reasonable compliance practice for a less-familiar, newer code family.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('8613a89d-6eb0-4b51-983b-3c8f5b3d6f7c', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 98, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable compliance/audit consideration specifically for RPM and CCM code billing, given their recurring, time-based, non-face-to-face nature?');
VALUES ('8613a89d-6eb0-4b51-983b-3c8f5b3d6f7c', '4a5199f4-f32f-4d31-ad62-b1439ff89efa', 'The recurring, non-face-to-face, time-based nature of RPM/CCM billing creates a documentation-verification challenge somewhat distinct from a single discrete face-to-face visit — contemporaneous, clear time/activity documentation is particularly important for these specific code families to support ongoing compliant billing.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('36040163-94c9-46c6-b253-2c2078cd87af', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 99, 'Which of the following best describes modifier GX, as distinguished from modifier GA?');
VALUES ('36040163-94c9-46c6-b253-2c2078cd87af', '094730e3-8d6c-4163-9991-8f8dd74d0aef', 'GX and GA both relate to ABNs but address different underlying reasons for expected non-coverage — GX for statutorily excluded/non-benefit services (voluntary ABN), GA for services expected to be denied as not medically necessary (required ABN) — a real distinction in what each modifier actually communicates to the payer.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');

VALUES ('17e5b97c-a075-400e-bf53-353f219ce0a6', '2b141fbc-9380-4f7a-92d5-8362b10dd931', 100, 'Which of the following best describes a reasonable general principle for approaching an unfamiliar modifier-selection question, based on the recurring themes established across surgical, telehealth, ABN-related, and NCCI-bypass modifiers across both practice papers?');
VALUES ('17e5b97c-a075-400e-bf53-353f219ce0a6', '26d57c0e-ff36-4da2-b971-3bf1c665d63c', 'This is the capstone synthesis for modifier usage across both CPT practice papers — correct modifier selection consistently depends on matching the SPECIFIC documented circumstance to a modifier''s own precise defined meaning, not selecting for reimbursement optimization, treating modifiers as optional, or over-applying them indiscriminately.', 'Modifiers & NCCI — authored from standard CPT modifier definitions covering telehealth and ABN-related modifiers, and CMS NCCI edit concepts; not copied from the copyrighted CPT codebook or an official exam bank.');
